The relevant classification theorem says:

1. either the conformal action is **inessential**, i.e. the local conformal pseudogroup preserves some Lorentzian metric in the conformal class \([g]\);
2. or \((M,g)\) is **conformally flat**;
3. or, in the remaining essential non-flat case, \((M,g)\) is **locally conformally equivalent to a homogeneous plane wave** (equivalently, an isometrically locally homogeneous plane wave).

Among the choices, this is exactly **(E)**. The others are too strong or too weak:
- (A) incorrectly requires an Einstein metric,
- (B) incorrectly restricts to locally symmetric plane waves,
- (C) unnecessarily shifts to the universal cover,
- (D) is too broad since not every plane wave occurs.

\boxed{E}

The stated regularity result is a **local Morrey estimate at the same exponent \(p\)**:

- it is **local** (\(\Omega'\Subset\Omega\)), not global on all of \(\Omega\));
- it gives \(\nabla u \in L^{p,\lambda}_{\mathrm{loc}}(\Omega)\);
- the theorem is not merely the trivial \(L^p_{\mathrm{loc}}\) fact;
- and it does not assert the stronger family of estimates in \(L^{q,\lambda}\) for all \(q>p\).

So the matching choice is **(C)**.

\[
\boxed{C}
